WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.718
A trail has numbered platforms 0 through n - 1 and an exit at position n. Platform i

2
00:00:05.718 --> 00:00:12.076
requires costs[i] lantern tokens when you leave it. You may start on platform 0 or platform 1 without

3
00:00:12.076 --> 00:00:18.706
an initial fee. Each move advances one or two positions. Return the minimum total tokens required to reach

4
00:00:18.706 --> 00:00:24.639
the exit. The exit has no fee. You pay for every platform you leave, including your starting platform.

5
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.931
Start at platform 0, pay 4 to move to platform 2, then pay 2 to reach exit 4.

6
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.430
Start at platform 1 and pay 3 to reach the exit.

7
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.074
Many routes reach the same platform. Once the cheapest arrival cost is known, more expensive arrivals need not

8
00:00:48.074 --> 00:00:48.872
be reconsidered.

9
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.014
Build the minimum arrival cost from left to right. Only the two preceding values are needed for the

10
00:00:55.014 --> 00:00:55.975
next transition.

11
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.195
I define the state as the minimum cost to arrive at a position. The last move comes from

12
00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:08.217
one of two earlier platforms, including that platform’s departure fee. Computing each state once removes

13
00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:12.866
repeated recursion, and keeping only two states reduces extra space to constant.

14
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:26.960
The free starting choices give dp[0] = 0 and dp[1] = 0. For [4,9,2,7], dp[2] = min(9,4) =

15
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:27.451
4.

16
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:36.147
dp[3] = min(dp[2]+2, dp[1]+9) = 6.

17
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:46.239
The exit is position 4: dp[4] = min(dp[3]+7, dp[2]+2) = 6.

18
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.460
O(n). O(1)
